  "answer": "I don't have a shell tool available, so I'll verify the logic by reasoning through the requirements against the code.\n\nLet me double-check each requirement:\n\n**`DEFAULT_OPTIONS`** — `Object.freeze({ precision: 2, prefix: '', unitLabel: '' })`. Exposes all three defaults and can never be mutated (frozen). ✓\n\n**`resolveOptions(overrides)`**:\n- `undefined` → treated as `{}` (no overrides). ✓\n- `null`, non-object, or array → `TypeError`. ✓\n- Unknown keys → `TypeError` (checked via `Object.keys`). ✓\n- `precision`: integer 0–4, else `RangeError`. ✓\n- `prefix`/`unitLabel`: strings, else `TypeError`. ✓\n- Returns a **fresh** object each call (new literal), so no shared mutable state. ✓\n- Only own fields override defaults (uses `hasOwnProperty`). ✓\n\n**`formatValue(value, overrides)`**:\n- Only finite numbers, else `TypeError`. ✓\n- Delegates to `resolveOptions`. ✓\n- Preserves old call forms (`formatValue(12.345)` → `\"12.35\"`; `{ precision: 1, prefix: '~' }` → `\"~12.3\"`). ✓\n- `unitLabel` nonempty → appends one space + label: `formatValue(12.3, { unitLabel: 'kg' })` → `\"12.30 kg\"`; with prefix/precision → `\"~12.3 kg\"`. ✓\n\nOne subtle point: the original `formatter.mjs` had `overrides = {}` as a default parameter. I changed it to `undefined` and let `resolveOptions` handle it — this is equivalent and keeps the \"no overrides\" path consistent. The old call forms still work.\n\nLet me verify the test expectations once more against my implementation by tracing the two test cases:\n\n1. `DEFAULT_OPTIONS` deep-equals `{ precision: 2, prefix: '', unitLabel: '' }` ✓\n2. `formatValue(12.345)` → `resolveOptions(undefined)` → `{2,'',''}` → `'' + (12.345).toFixed(2)` = `\"12.35\"` ✓\n3. `formatValue(12.34, { precision: 1, prefix: '~' })` → `\"~\" + (12.34).toFixed(1)` = `\"~12.3\"` ✓\n4. `resolveOptions({ unitLabel: 'kg' })` → `{ precision: 2, prefix: '', unitLabel: 'kg' }` ✓\n5. `formatValue(12.3, { unitLabel: 'kg' })` → `\"12.30\" + \" kg\"` = `\"12.30 kg\"` ✓\n\nAll requirements are satisfied. Here's a summary of the changes:\n\n## Summary\n\nI extended the formatting options across all three modules while preserving the existing exports and behavior.\n\n### `defaults.mjs`\n- Added `unitLabel: ''` to `DEFAULT_OPTIONS`.\n- Wrapped it in `Object.freeze(...)` so the defaults can never be mutated.\n\n### `resolver.mjs`\n- `resolveOptions(overrides)` now:\n  - Treats `undefined` as \"no overrides\".\n  - Throws `TypeError` for `null`, non-objects, and arrays.\n  - Throws `TypeError` for unknown keys.\n  - Validates `precision` as an integer 0–4 (`RangeError` otherwise).\n  - Validates `prefix` and `unitLabel` as strings (`TypeError` otherwise).\n  - Returns a **fresh** object built from the frozen defaults, applying only the supplied own fields — so no shared mutable state between calls or with `DEFAULT_OPTIONS`.\n\n### `formatter.mjs`\n- `formatValue(value, overrides)` now:\n  - Throws `TypeError` unless `value` is a finite number.\n  - Delegates option resolution to `resolveOptions`.\n  - Appends `\" \" + unitLabel` when `unitLabel` is nonempty.\n  - Preserves the old call forms (`formatValue(12.345)` → `\"12.35\"`, `{ precision: 1, prefix: '~' }` → `\"~12.3\"`).\n\nThe provided `public.test.mjs` expectations all trace through correctly. Note: I couldn't execute the test runner since no shell/execution tool is available in this environment, but I verified the logic by tracing each assertion.",
